      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point",
      "problem": "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(e,2).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{e}=4-e\\end{aligned}\n$$",
