    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132604",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point",
      "problem": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$",
